Our Montessori classrooms are organized into multi-age cohorts, in which students have the ability to learn from one another, teach one another, and work with the same teacher long enough to promote deep understanding of individual differences. We offer a rich academic curriculum without the stresses of a standardized delivery timetable, enabling children to satisfy[ their intellectual appetites and] initiate work that is unique to them. Because students stay with the same teacher for several years, lessons and follow-up work can be responsive[ to the interests and needs] of the[ children].
Being an independent school, we are not bound by the strict chronological groupings and "September only" transitions that are commonplace in U.S. schools. The Montessori method informs a true responsiveness[ to the] development of the individual. Teacher, parents[ and] child are able to make crucial educational decisions based[ on the] unique[ needs] of each child.
We call our teachers "[gu]ides", because our work is not so much to tell children what we know as to assist[ their natural] urge to become high functioning members of society. Montessori guides are specially trained to understand the particular developmental tasks and sensitivities of the age[ group cohorts] they work with. New lessons and materials are introduced in ways that promote an appetite for more. Montessori[ learning] environments are prepared to serve[ the] development of deep concentration, self-confidence, initiative and self[-]regulated social interaction.

